Subject: wim: bound chunk and resource reads from the WIM

The offsets in a compressed resource's chunk table come from boot.wim and
were only checked to lie within the resource. A chunk whose next offset
is lower than its own gave a wrapped, huge length; any length became a
stack VLA (uint8_t zbuf[len]) that the read then filled with data from
the WIM. On UEFI the stack is small and unguarded, so a crafted boot.wim
could write its own bytes over the stack or over memory below it, before
any signature check of what wimboot loads next (a Secure Boot bypass for
any signed wimboot). Found by review (docs/SECURITY_REVIEW-0.4.0.md W1)
and confirmed by fuzzing (build/wimboot/fuzz).

Now:
- chunk offsets must not decrease and a stored chunk is at most
  WIM_CHUNK_LEN bytes (no writer stores a chunk longer than its data);
  it is read into a static WIM_CHUNK_LEN buffer, not a VLA;
- the resource and file bounds checks cannot overflow;
- an uncompressed resource must not claim more data than it stores
  (reads past the end of the WIM file otherwise);
- a compressed resource's chunk count must fit in an unsigned int;
- directory entry lengths must not wrap the walk back to an earlier
  entry (a loop: wimboot would hang);
- wimpatch.c: the lengths it builds the patched WIM from (lookup table,
  boot metadata, injection directory) and the patched length are kept
  within one virtual disk file (4 GiB), so no region offset can wrap.

A valid WIM reads exactly as before. beast-boot (build/wimboot/); to be
reported to and offered upstream.
